Photo courtesy: Shutterstock', 'Chris Murphy Accuses AI Companies of Buying Rare Books, Digitizing Them and Disposing of Originals: ''Destroy Human Creativity''

Senator Chris Murphy warns that AI companies are buying and destroying rare books for training, potentially undermining human creativity.

Sen. Chris Murphy (D-Conn.) warned that artificial intelligence companies could be undermining human creativity by acquiring rare books, digitizing their contents for AI training and disposing of the physical originals.

## Murphy Warns of AI Book Training

On Tuesday, Murphy made the comments in a post on X while sharing a clip of himself discussing what he described as efforts by AI companies to acquire and digitize scarce books.

"The AI companies claim their technology will enhance human creativity. But they are quietly undertaking a massive campaign to destroy human creativity," Murphy wrote.

In the video, Murphy alleged that AI companies are "literally buying up millions of books," including rare works with only one or a few remaining copies, before scanning and disposing of them.

## Anthropic’s Alleged Rare Book Project

Murphy specifically pointed to Anthropic and its "Project Panama," an effort documented in court filings that involved purchasing millions of physical books, removing their bindings, scanning their pages and disposing of the originals. Murphy raised concerns that such practices could put scarce and potentially rare works at risk.

Murphy also claimed the companies are particularly interested in books created before 2022 because they represent work produced without AI assistance.

"These were wholly human-created works," he said, calling the alleged practice "especially perverse."

Murphy argued that destroying rare physical works could make it harder for future generations to access examples of original human creativity and distinguish them from AI-generated content.

He also called for congressional action, saying lawmakers could establish "common sense guard rails" around AI development.

"The pace of this development, the pace of this innovation is frightening," Murphy said, arguing that AI companies should operate "a little bit slower and a little bit more responsibly."

The AI companies claim their technology will enhance human creativity. But they are quietly undertaking a massive campaign to destroy human creativity.

Let me tell you the story of the AI companies'' effort to feed rare books into their AI models and then destroy them. pic.twitter.com/6ZwQXcDCVb

— Chris Murphy 🟧 (@ChrisMurphyCT) August 25, 2026

