The Technology Select Sector SPDR Fund (NYSE:XLK) held a 0.2% gain despite the Nvidia-related caution in the index.

Precious-metals equities took the worst of it, as the VanEck Gold Miners ETF (NYSE:GDX) tumbled 2.7% alongside bullion and AngloGold Ashanti plc (NYSE:AU) dropped 4.3%.

The Invesco WilderHill Clean Energy ETF (NYSE:PBW) shed 1.5%, while the SPDR S&P Insurance ETF (NYSE:KIE) rose 0.5% and the VanEck Agribusiness ETF (NYSE:MOO) added 0.4%.

## Pipeline Operators — The Day’s Cleanest Trade

DT Midstream Inc. (NYSE:DTM) rose 5.1%, The Williams Companies Inc. (NYSE:WMB) gained 4.6% and Kinder Morgan Inc. (NYSE:KMI) added 4.1% as natural gas jumped. C.H. Robinson Worldwide Inc. (NASDAQ:CHRW) climbed 5.3% and The J. M. Smucker Company (NYSE:SJM) rose 4.0%.

Semtech Corp. (NASDAQ:SMTC) led the Russell 1000 with an 8.1% surge after reporting record fiscal second-quarter net sales of $341.9 million late Tuesday, up 33% year-over-year, with data center revenue hitting a record $100 million on 800G and 1.6T demand. Semtech guided next-quarter revenue to roughly $410 million at the midpoint, about 15% above consensus.

TTM Technologies Inc. (NASDAQ:TTMI) rose 6.6%, trading in sympathy with the AI-hardware complex ahead of Nvidia’s print. Peers moved with it, as Arista Networks Inc. (NYSE:ANET) gained 3.9% and Lumentum Holdings Inc. (NASDAQ:LITE) added 3.8%.

Summit Therapeutics Inc. (NASDAQ:SMMT) climbed 6.6%. The move follows a roughly 25% one-month drawdown and comes ahead of the FDA’s decision on ivonescimab expected by mid-November.

## Retail Delivers Session’s Other Upside

Bath & Body Works Inc. (NYSE:BBWI) jumped 6.4% after posting adjusted earnings of 62 cents per share against a 25-cent consensus and lifting full-year adjusted EPS guidance to $2.60-$2.80 from $2.40-$2.65, marking its first direct net sales growth since 2021 – enough to override a third-quarter forecast of 7 to 12 cents versus 26 cents expected.

The Gap Inc. (NYSE:GAP) rallied 5.7%, riding the read-across into its own second-quarter report due Thursday after the close. DICK’S Sporting Goods Inc. (NYSE:DKS) tacked on 3.2%.

The day’s most brutal reaction? Dycom Industries Inc. (NYSE:DY). The provider of specialty contracting services’ stock price dipped 11.3% despite record fiscal second-quarter contract revenue of about $2 billion, up 45.6%, adjusted EPS of $5.29, backlog of $12.24 billion, up 53.2%, and a raised full-year outlook.

Zoom Communications Inc. (NASDAQ:ZM) tumbled 7.8% even after beating with adjusted EPS of $1.55 against $1.48 expected and raising full-year fiscal 2027 revenue, EPS, and free cash flow guidance. Total revenue growth of just 4.9% left investors underwhelmed on the pace of AI monetization.

Moderna Inc. (NASDAQ:MRNA) dropped 7.4% as profit-taking extended into a fourth session following last week’s 177% single-day surge on late-stage melanoma data for its personalized cancer vaccine. Caris Life Sciences Inc. (NASDAQ:CAI) fell 5.9% with no confirmed catalyst on the tape, last reporting on Aug. 5.

Elsewhere, Intuit Inc. (NASDAQ:INTU) slid 4.5% after guiding fiscal 2027 revenue to $23.28 billion-$23.51 billion, implying 9% to 10% growth, with Mailchimp seen flat to down 1%.

Boston Scientific Corp. (NYSE:BSX) lost 4.6%, and Eli Lilly and Co. (NYSE:LLY) fell 3.5%, weighing on healthcare.

Crypto-linked equities also came under pressure, with Bitcoin down 0.8% near $78,000. Coinbase Global Inc. (NASDAQ:COIN) fell 3.8%, Strategy Inc. (NASDAQ:MSTR) lost 3.7%, and IREN Ltd. (NASDAQ:IREN) dropped 5.5%.

