SELECT d.document_id, (SELECT publisher_id FROM publisher WHERE canonical_key = 'benzinga'), 'media_report', 'How Does Congress Feel About Nvidia Stock Ahead of Q2 Earnings? Here’s How Many Have Sold in 2026', false, 'Chris Katje', 'Ahead of Nvidia''s earnings report, here''s a look at whether Congress is mostly buying or selling Nvidia stock in 2026.', 'Nvidia Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) stock has been one of the top-performing companies by return over the last 5, 10, 15, and 20 years. Heading into second-quarter financial results Wednesday, members of Congress who regularly trade stocks seem to be mixed on what to expect.

## Congress Trading Nvidia Stock

The Benzinga Government Trades page tracks Nvidia stock buying and selling over various time periods.

So far in 2026, there have been 17 buy orders and 23 sell orders from members of Congress. This could signal that members of Congress are more likely to take profits this year and to see slower growth from Nvidia going forward.

The majority of the transactions in Nvidia stock in 2026 are in the $1,000 to $15,000 range.

Here are the largest buy orders of Nvidia stock by members of Congress in 2026:

- $50,000 to $100,000: one time
- $250,000 to $500,000: one time

Here are the largest sell orders of Nvidia stock by members of Congress in 2026:

- $15,000 to $50,000: three times
- $50,000 to $100,000: one time
- $100,000 to $250,000: two times

So not only are there more Nvidia sales this year, but there are also more sales of the larger transaction range.

Looking at the most recent transactions for May, June, and July, there were nine sales and five purchases. The sales ranged from $235,000 to $675,000, with the buys ranging from $5,000 to $75,000.

Recent transactions show much more conviction in selling than buying.

Read Also: Nvidia Stock: 84% of Benzinga Viewers Have Owned It. Here’s How Many Still Do.

## Nvidia Earnings Expectations

Nvidia stock has a strong history of beating analyst estimates in recent quarters, but that hasn’t always translated to a higher stock price.

The company has beaten earnings-per-share estimates in 18 of the last 20 quarters and revenue estimates in 19 of the last 20 quarters. The current streaks of beating earnings per share and revenue are 14 and 15 quarters, respectively.

The stock has fallen after five of the last six earnings reports.

Analysts expect Nvidia to report second-quarter revenue of $92.01 billion, up from $46.74 billion in last year’s second quarter, according to data from Benzinga Pro.

Analysts expect Nvidia to report second-quarter earnings per share of $2.09, up from $1.04 in last year’s second quarter.

Read Also: House Passes Congressional Stock Trading Ban — Then The White House Gets Community Noted Over It

