SELECT d.document_id, (SELECT publisher_id FROM publisher WHERE canonical_key = 'benzinga'), 'media_report', 'Crowdstrike Earnings Prediction Market Preview: What Will George Kurtz Say About AI?', false, 'Daragh Thomas', 'CrowdStrike reports earnings as traders bet on Falcon Flex, Mythos and Nvidia, while investors watch whether AI is accelerating net new ARR.', 'CrowdStrike Holdings Inc. (NASDAQ:CRWD) reports fiscal second-quarter results after Wednesday’s closing bell.

Polymarket traders put the odds of adjusted earnings topping 29 cents per share at 80%. The livelier action is on Kalshi, where traders are betting on which words CEO George Kurtz and his team will say.

CrowdStrike guided revenue to about $1.44 billion, but investors may care more about net new ARR. The company expects $284 million to $286 million, up from $256 million last quarter, offering a clearer test of whether Kurtz’s AI security pitch is translating into new business.

## What Kalshi Predicts CrowdStrike Will Say

“Falcon Flex” leads the board at 98%. Flex, CrowdStrike’s flexible subscription model, passed $1.9 billion in ending ARR last quarter, up more than 99% year over year.

“Consolidation,” at 86%, is the thesis behind Flex: replacing multiple security vendors with one Falcon platform.

“Mythos” trades at 85%. Kurtz called Anthropic’s Mythos release an “inflection point” last quarter, arguing that more capable AI can both find and weaponize vulnerabilities. CrowdStrike has since built Project QuiltWorks around defending against that threat.

“Nvidia” trades at 80%. CrowdStrike has embedded Falcon protection into Nvidia Corp.‘s (NASDAQ:NVDA) AI-agent infrastructure and hired former Nvidia engineering leader Bartley Richardson as its AI chief.

“AWS” trades at 77%. CrowdStrike teamed with Amazon this month on an AI-agent red-teaming challenge focused on threats such as prompt injection and agent hijacking.

“OpenAI” sits at 74%. CrowdStrike is a launch partner in OpenAI’s Trusted Access for Cyber program, while both companies are also prominent sponsors of next week’s Fal.Con.

“Acquisition” trades at 72%. CrowdStrike has been an active buyer, most recently agreeing to acquire XM Cyber’s intellectual property from Schwarz Digits. “XM Cyber” itself trades at 63%.

“Cerebras” sits at 61%. CrowdStrike partnered with Cerebras (NASDAQ:CBRS) in July to speed up Falcon’s AI threat detection, with the idea that AI-powered attacks increasingly require defenses that can respond at machine speed.

## What Kalshi Predicts CrowdStrike Will Skip

“AI Detection” and “Continuous Identity” both trade at 45%. Falcon AI Detection and Response grew more than 250% sequentially last quarter with a $50 million-plus pipeline, while Continuous Identity controls what autonomous AI agents can access.

“China” trades at just 22%, even though CrowdStrike research links China-nexus adversaries to more than 58% of state-sponsored targeted intrusions against the technology sector.

“Gen AI,” at 6%, may say the most: CrowdStrike’s vocabulary has moved on to agents and agentic AI.

## Reading the Board

Kurtz ended last quarter calling accelerating ARR “the AI tailwind in action.” Investors will want to see that tailwind in net new ARR, not just hear about it on stage at Fal.Con.

The beat may be the easy part. The bigger question is whether AI is expanding CrowdStrike’s market fast enough to support Kurtz’s pitch that cybersecurity is becoming critical AI infrastructure.

Image: Shutterstock

Kalshi and Benzinga have an existing data collaboration agreement.

