\"NYSE\\" href=\\"https://www.benzinga.com/quote/CRM\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">(NYSE:<a class=\\"ticker\\" href=\\"https://www.benzinga.com/quote/CRM\\">CRM</a>)</a>, ATM implied volatility climbs roughly 90% over the final 5 trading days before a report — close to doubling from where it stood a week earlier. And the ramp is heavily back-loaded: roughly two-thirds of the climb arrives in the final 2 sessions.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<figure class=\\"wp-block-image size-full\\"><img loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" width=\\"1200\\" height=\\"298\\" src=\\"https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233831/fig1_crm_radar-1.png\\" alt=\\"Salesforce at-the-money implied volatility rising into earnings: current cycle vs. historical median\\" class=\\"wp-image-38081\\" srcset=\\"https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233831/fig1_crm_radar-1.png 1200w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233831/fig1_crm_radar-1-300x75.png 300w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233831/fig1_crm_radar-1-1024x254.png 1024w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233831/fig1_crm_radar-1-768x191.png 768w\\" sizes=\\"(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px\\" /></figure>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><em>Salesforce (CRM): at-the-money implied volatility into past earnings reports. Black = median historical path (right axis, percentage change from 5 sessions out); purple = the cycle into the Aug. 26, 2026 report, captured hours before the release. Source: EarningsWatcher</em></p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>This is not one lucky quarter — the same climb shows up in every recent cycle:</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<figure class=\\"wp-block-image size-full\\"><img loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" width=\\"1200\\" height=\\"455\\" src=\\"https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233807/fig2_crm_bars-1.png\\" alt=\\"Salesforce 5-day pre-earnings implied volatility climb for each of the last 8 reports\\" class=\\"wp-image-38080\\" srcset=\\"https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233807/fig2_crm_bars-1.png 1200w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233807/fig2_crm_bars-1-300x114.png 300w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233807/fig2_crm_bars-1-1024x388.png 1024w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26233807/fig2_crm_bars-1-768x291.png 768w\\" sizes=\\"(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px\\" /></figure>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><em>Salesforce&#8217;s 5-day pre-earnings IV climb, report by report: between +63% and +111% in each of the last 8 cycles. Source: EarningsWatcher</em></p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\"wp-block-heading\\">Not Every Stock Rushes The Same</h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>Intel Corporation <a href=\\"https://www.benzinga.com/quote/INTC\\" target=\\"_blank\\" class=\\"ticker-link\\" data-ticker=\\"INTC\\" data-exchange=\\"NASDAQ\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">(NASDAQ:<a class=\\"ticker\\" href=\\"https://www.benzinga.com/quote/INTC\\">INTC</a>)</a> shows one of the steepest profiles in the dataset: a median climb of roughly 150% over the final week. NVIDIA Corporation <a href=\\"https://www.benzinga.com/quote/NVDA\\" target=\\"_blank\\" class=\\"ticker-link\\" data-ticker=\\"NVDA\\" data-exchange=\\"NASDAQ\\" rel=\\"noopener\\">(NASDAQ:<a class=\\"ticker\\" href=\\"https://www.benzinga.com/quote/NVDA\\">NVDA</a>)</a>, one of the most-traded options names in the market, ramps about 70% over the same window. Same market, same 5 days — one stock&#8217;s volatility builds at twice the pace of the other&#8217;s. The rush is a per-stock fingerprint, not a universal constant.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<figure class=\\"wp-block-image size-full\\"><img loading=\\"lazy\\" decoding=\\"async\\" width=\\"1200\\" height=\\"480\\" src=\\"https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26192120/fig3_intc_nvda.png\\" alt=\\"Intel vs. NVIDIA median implied volatility paths into earnings\\" class=\\"wp-image-38073\\" srcset=\\"https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26192120/fig3_intc_nvda.png 1200w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26192120/fig3_intc_nvda-300x120.png 300w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26192120/fig3_intc_nvda-1024x410.png 1024w, https://contributor-assets.benzinga.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/26192120/fig3_intc_nvda-768x307.png 768w\\" sizes=\\"(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px\\" /></figure>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><em>Intel vs. NVIDIA: median path of at-the-money implied volatility into each stock&#8217;s past earnings reports (percentage change from 5 sessions out). Source: EarningsWatcher</em></p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\"wp-block-heading\\">The Math: IV Rush Vs. Theta Decay</h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>An at-the-money straddle&#8217;s value is roughly proportional to implied volatility times the square root of time remaining — and before earnings those two forces pull in opposite directions.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>Take a $10 ATM straddle with 5 trading days to expiry. If 2 days pass and implied volatility stays flat, decay alone takes it to about $7.75 — down 22% with the stock going nowhere. Run the same 2 days with IV climbing 40%, the kind of jump the back-loaded ramp routinely delivers, and the straddle sits near $10.85 instead — again holding the stock price constant. In this simplified example, IV must rise roughly 29% merely to offset the loss of 2 trading days — so a 40% rise in IV translates into a gain of only about 8.5% in the straddle&#8217;s value, not 40%. That tug-of-war is at the core of earnings-week options pricing: the practical question is whether the IV ramp is strong enough to offset the time disappearing from the contract.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The ramp is measured at the money, but the earnings premium affects much of the expiration&#8217;s volatility surface, so out-of-the-money strikes generally show rising IV as well. That is why&nbsp;straddles and strangles&nbsp;feel it differently: a straddle carries the most direct exposure to the ramp at the highest cost, while a strangle costs less but requires a larger move before either leg gains intrinsic value.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>Both structures are exposed to the same contest between rising IV and disappearing time, just in different proportions — and calendar spreads go a step further, splitting the two forces across different expirations. For anyone trading earnings week, the practical upshot is that&nbsp;<em>when</em>&nbsp;you enter matters as much as what you buy.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\"wp-block-heading\\">The Caveats</h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The cleanest historical estimates come from names with liquid options and a sufficiently long earnings history, and market-wide volatility shifts the baseline from quarter to quarter. Historical medians describe typical behavior — they are not a prediction for any single report.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<h2 class=\\"wp-block-heading\\">The Bottom Line</h2>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p>The IV crush is the second act. Before the report, implied volatility follows a measurable, repeating, stock-specific path — and as the math above shows, rising IV does not automatically mean a rising option price, nor does a high IV reading alone mean an option is mispriced. Where a name sits inside its own cycle is the context that makes its price readable. That is what turns earnings option prices from a surprise into a pattern.</p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><em>Disclosure: The author operates EarningsWatcher, the analytics platform whose data is referenced in this article. The author holds no positions in the securities mentioned.</em></p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><em>Disclaimer: EarningsWatcher is a publisher, not a registered broker-dealer or investment advisor. The information in this article is for informational and educational purposes only and should not be considered personalized investment advice.</em></p>\\n\\n\\n\\n<p><strong><em>Benzinga Disclaimer: This article is from an unpaid external contributor. It does not represent Benzinga&#8217;s reporting and has not been edited for content or accuracy.</em></strong></p>", "created_at": "2026-08-27T17:20:30Z", "headline": "The IV Crush Is Only Half The Story: How Options Get So Expensive Before Earnings", "id": 61474226, "images": [{"size": "large", "url": "https://cdn.benzinga.com/files/images/story/2026/08/27/fig3-intc-nvda.png?width=2048&height=1536"}, {"size": "small", "url": "https://cdn.benzinga.com/files/images/story/2026/08/27/fig3-intc-nvda.png?width=1024&height=768"}, {"size": "thumb", "url": "https://cdn.benzinga.com/files/images/story/2026/08/27/fig3-intc-nvda.png?width=250&height=187"}], "source": "benzinga", "summary": "Before the IV crush comes the IV rush. Data on Salesforce, Intel and NVIDIA shows how implied volatility climbs into earnings — and why option prices don&#39;t follow.", "symbols": ["CRM", "INTC", "NVDA"], "updated_at": "2026-08-27T17:20:30Z", "url": "https://www.benzinga.com/Opinion/26/08/61474226/the-iv-crush-is-only-half-the-story-how-options-get-so-expensive-before-earnings"}'::jsonb, '93a03305cf067c0ec3557810afb535e7602788aa7c59673bfa7d880d1dc5b997', NULL, NULL, NULL, '["file:/Users/zuttome/LocalDocs/Learning/College/NUS/MTech-AIS/IRS/GC1/always-ignore/architecture-book/evaluation/data/alpaca_windows/0001045810-26-000073.json", "raw/alpaca/articles/61474226/18b1b0a90019.json"]'::jsonb, '2026-10-19T04:46:36.878379+00:00'::timestamptz
